.TH CTPOOL_TIMED_SUBMIT_FUTURE 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_timed_submit_future \- submit a future task with a relative timeout
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

ccol_retval_t ctpool_timed_submit_future(ctpool pool,
                                         void *(*fn)(void *),
                                         void *arg,
                                         uint64_t timeout_us,
                                         ctpool_future **out);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates a future and submits it to the pool. It waits at most
timeout_us microseconds, measured from the call, for a free slot on a
bounded queue. The library converts the timeout to an absolute deadline
with CLOCK_MONOTONIC. A change to the wall clock of the system, by an
administrator, NTP, or the resume of a virtual machine, therefore does
not make the wait longer or shorter.

A timeout_us of 0 makes this call the same as ctpool_try_submit_future,
which does not wait.

A timeout too large to form a deadline, such as UINT64_MAX, is a wait
for as long as the queue stays full. The deadline saturates at the
latest representable time, and never wraps into the past.

On success the function sets *out to a new ctpool_future handle with a
reference count of 2. The caller holds one reference, and the task in
the queue holds the other. The caller must call ctpool_future_free
exactly one time when it is done.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
pool        Thread pool handle
fn          Task function returning void* (must not be NULL)
arg         The library gives it to fn (may be NULL)
timeout_us  Longest time to wait, in microseconds (0 means do not wait)
out         Receives the future handle on success. The function sets it
            to NULL on a failure (must not be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success            - task enqueued; *out set to the future handle.
ccol_invalid_args       - pool is CTPOOL_INVALID or a stale/already-
                          destroyed handle, or fn or out is NULL.
ccol_not_enough_memory  - future or task allocation failed.
ccol_not_permitted      - pool is shutting down.
ccol_container_full     - timeout_us is 0 and the queue is full.
ccol_timed_out          - timeout elapsed before a slot became free.
ccol_unexpected_failure - clock_gettime or pthread_cond_timedwait
                          returned an unexpected error.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_submit_future (3),
.BR ctpool_try_submit_future (3),
.BR ctpool_future_get (3),
.BR ctpool_future_free (3)
